1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,959
A customer asks your AI assistant about refunds.

2
00:00:04,019 --> 00:00:08,751
It answers, confidently: ninety days. Your policy says thirty.

3
00:00:08,811 --> 00:00:10,827
Would you even know it happened?

4
00:00:11,077 --> 00:00:13,560
Fix found: Langfuse.

5
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:17,193
Think of it as a flight recorder for your AI app.

6
00:00:17,253 --> 00:00:20,980
It's open source, with more than thirty-six thousand stars on

7
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:21,611
GitHub.

8
00:00:21,861 --> 00:00:24,313
You add a few lines of code to your app.

9
00:00:24,373 --> 00:00:28,647
From then on, every question, every answer, and every step in

10
00:00:28,647 --> 00:00:30,752
between is saved as a trace.

11
00:00:30,812 --> 00:00:34,273
Later, you can open any conversation and see exactly what

12
00:00:34,273 --> 00:00:35,011
happened.

13
00:00:35,261 --> 00:00:39,295
One. Trace a complaint. Find the exact conversation, the prompt

14
00:00:39,295 --> 00:00:42,186
that was sent, and what the model said back.

15
00:00:42,246 --> 00:00:46,586
Two. Watch your spend. It tracks tokens and cost for every call,

16
00:00:46,586 --> 00:00:49,395
with prices built in for popular models.

17
00:00:49,455 --> 00:00:53,572
Three. Score the answers. Collect thumbs up and thumbs down from

18
00:00:53,572 --> 00:00:57,630
users, or let another model grade them, so the bad answers rise

19
00:00:57,630 --> 00:00:58,538
to the top.

20
00:00:58,788 --> 00:01:00,376
Now, the catch.

21
00:01:00,436 --> 00:01:04,765
The core is MIT licensed, but a few enterprise features sit in

22
00:01:04,765 --> 00:01:07,650
their own folder and need a license key.

23
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:11,922
And self-hosting means running several services: a web app, a

24
00:01:11,922 --> 00:01:16,329
worker, two databases, a cache, and file storage. Or you pay for

25
00:01:16,329 --> 00:01:18,856
their cloud, which has a free tier.

26
00:01:19,106 --> 00:01:22,525
It's worth it once real customers talk to your AI

27
00:01:22,585 --> 00:01:25,855
For a weekend prototype, it's probably more than you need.

28
00:01:25,915 --> 00:01:29,567
Want an alternative? Take a look at Arize Phoenix.

29
00:01:29,817 --> 00:01:33,368
To try it, clone the repo and run docker compose up. It's

30
00:01:33,368 --> 00:01:36,512
running on your own machine in about five minutes.

31
00:01:36,572 --> 00:01:40,201
What problem should we fix next? Tell us in the comments.

